🔹 Approach — Backtracking

Sudoku is a constraint satisfaction problem.

We use:

1️⃣ Try placing numbers from '1' to '9'
2️⃣ Check if valid:

Not in same row

Not in same column

Not in same 3×3 box

3️⃣ If valid → place it
4️⃣ Recur
5️⃣ If dead end → backtrack (reset to '.')
🔹 Step-by-Step Algorithm
solve(board):
   for each cell:
       if cell is empty:
           for digit from 1 to 9:
               if valid:
                   place digit
                   if solve(board):
                       return true
                   else:
                       undo (backtrack)
           return false
   return true
🔹 How 3×3 Box is Calculated (Important Interview Point)

To check box:

boxRow = 3 * (row / 3) + i / 3
boxCol = 3 * (col / 3) + i % 3

This maps index 0-8 across the 3×3 subgrid.

Interviewers love this part.

🔹 Java Code (Optimal & Clean)
public class SudokuSolver {

    public void solveSudoku(char[][] board) {
        solve(board);
    }

    private boolean solve(char[][] board) {

        for (int row = 0; row < 9; row++) {
            for (int col = 0; col < 9; col++) {

                if (board[row][col] == '.') {

                    for (char num = '1'; num <= '9'; num++) {

                        if (isValid(board, row, col, num)) {

                            board[row][col] = num;

                            if (solve(board))
                                return true;
                            else
                                board[row][col] = '.'; 
                        }
                    }
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    private boolean isValid(char[][] board, int row, int col, char num) {

        for (int i = 0; i < 9; i++) {

            if (board[i][col] == num) return false;

            if (board[row][i] == num) return false;

            int boxRow = 3 * (row / 3) + i / 3;
            int boxCol = 3 * (col / 3) + i % 3;

            if (board[boxRow][boxCol] == num)
                return false;
        }

        return true;
    }
}
🔹 Line-by-Line Explanation
solveSudoku()

Starts recursion.

solve()

Outer loops:

for row
   for col

We scan board.

If empty cell found:

for num = 1 to 9

Try all digits.

If valid:

board[row][col] = num

Then:

if (solve(board))
   return true

If it fails:

board[row][col] = '.'

That is BACKTRACKING.

isValid()

Checks:

Column conflict

Row conflict

3×3 box conflict

Returns false if conflict.

🔹 Time Complexity

Worst case:

O(9^(n²))

But actual runtime much faster due to pruning.

🔹 Space Complexity

O(1) (Board size fixed 9x9)

Recursion stack at most 81 calls deep.

